{
  "ID": "3.2",
  "Context/Background": "To find novel treatments and drug combinations that could effectively target colorectal cancer cells (CRCs) and lower the probability of disease relapse, we designed multiple different combinations between cisplatin, cannabidiol (CBD), and intermittent serum starvation (ISS) on colorectal cancer cell lines. We analyzed differentially expressed genes and affected pathways in colorectal cancer cell lines to understand further the potential molecular mechanisms behind the treatments and their interactions.",
  "Prompt": "Based on the results of the differential expression analysis of CBD vs DMSO in colorectal cancer cells, run a pathway overrepresentation analysis for statistically significant genes (up and down combined) against the Reactome database. Then, provide a discussion of any relevant affected pathways, highlighting important up and down regulated genes in each.  Focus on any mechanisms by which CBD may alter cancer related pathways.",
  "mode": "Discovera",
  "llm_provider": "openai",
  "llm_model": "o4-mini",
  "run_date": "2025-12-14 16:18:52",
  "elapsed_seconds": 225.96,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "Here is the Reactome over‐representation analysis of all genes with adjusted p-value < 0.05 (n ≈ 2 051 Ensembl IDs) from the CBD vs DMSO contrast in colorectal cancer cells.  We filtered for padj < 0.05, ran ORA against Reactome (2024), and report the top pathways sorted by adjusted p-value:\n\n1) Oncogene Induced Senescence  \n   – Overlap 16/35 genes (adj p = 1.30 × 10^–5)  \n   – Leading genes: RB1; CDKN2A; ETS1; ETS2; CDK6; TFDP1; UBB; CDK4; UBC; ID1; MDM2; MAPK1; MDM4; TP53; TNRC6A; TNRC6B  \n\n2) Transcriptional Regulation by RUNX3  \n   – Overlap 29/86 genes (adj p = 1.02 × 10^–6)  \n   – Leading genes: YAP1; NOTCH1; MAML2; TCF7; RBPJ; FOXO3; PSMA7; PSMB6; PSMD7; CCND1; UBB; PSMC5; MDM2; KRAS; TP53; …  \n\n3) Cytosolic tRNA Aminoacylation  \n   – Overlap 12/24 genes (adj p = 9.11 × 10^–5)  \n   – Leading genes: AIMP2; GARS1; AARS1; SARS1; NARS1; MARS1; IARS1; YARS1; CARS1; TARS1; DARS1; FARSB  \n\n4) FOXO-mediated Transcription of Cell Cycle Genes  \n   – Overlap 9/17 genes (adj p = 6.10 × 10^–4)  \n   – Leading genes: SMAD2; BTG1; CDKN1B; SMAD3; GADD45A; CAV1; CCNG2; FOXO3; KLF4  \n\n5) TP53 Regulates Transcription of Cell Cycle Genes  \n   – Overlap 19/49 genes (adj p = 1.33 × 10^–5)  \n   – Leading genes: BTG2; CDKN1B; PCNA; GADD45A; ARID3A; CCNA2; CCNB1; TFDP1; BAX; SFN; E2F4; TP53; …  \n\n6) Downregulation of TGF-β Receptor Signaling  \n   – Overlap 11/26 genes (adj p = 8.01 × 10^–4)  \n   – Leading genes: PPP1R15A; SMAD2; PPP1CC; XPO1; SMAD3; UBB; SMURF2; UBC; TGFBR1; SMAD7; TGFBR2  \n\n7) Constitutive Signaling by AKT1 E17K in Cancer  \n   – Overlap 11/26 genes (adj p = 8.01 × 10^–4)  \n   – Leading genes: NR4A1; GSK3A; CDKN1B; CREB1; CHUK; MAPKAP1; RPS6KB2; MDM2; RICTOR; FOXO3; MTOR  \n\n8) Toll-Like Receptor 3 (TLR3) Cascade  \n   – Overlap 31/106 genes (adj p = 9.03 × 10^–6)  \n   – Leading genes: ATF2; NLRC5; HMGB1; NOD2; AGER; TANK; RELA; IRAK1; UBB; MAPK1; UBC; JUN; CHUK; FOS; NFKB2; …  \n\nDiscussion  \n– A strong senescence signature (Oncogene Induced Senescence) emerges, driven by deregulation of core tumor-suppressors (RB1, TP53, CDKN2A) and CDK regulators (CDK4, CDK6).  This suggests CBD may trigger a senescence program in CRC cells via the p53–CDK–RB axis.  \n– Multiple p53-dependent cell‐cycle modules are enriched (“TP53 Regulates…G1/S DNA Damage Checkpoint,” “Transcription of Cell Cycle Genes”), with key effectors CDKN1B, BAX and PCNA pointing to both checkpoint arrest and apoptotic priming.  Notably TP53 itself and its negative regulators MDM2/MDM4 are in the leading edge.  \n– The FOXO pathway (SMAD2/3, FOXO3, KLF4) and RUNX3 transcription modules are over‐represented, indicating CBD may rewire TGF-β/SMAD → FOXO tumor-suppressive signaling.  Downregulation of TGF-β receptor signaling further underscores modulation of this axis.  \n– PI3K/AKT signaling (AKT1 E17K) and MAP kinase cascades (via MAPK1, CHUK, JUN, FOS) also appear, consistent with CBD’s reported effects on survival pathways.  Inhibition of AKT may relieve repression of FOXO3, reinforcing anti‐proliferative outcomes.  \n– Enrichment of cytosolic tRNA aminoacylation hints at broader translational control effects, aligning with reports that CBD can impair protein synthesis in cancer cells.  \n\nTogether, these data support a model in which CBD shifts colorectal cancer cells toward growth arrest and senescence by engaging p53 and FOXO tumor‐suppressive networks, dampening pro‐survival AKT/MAPK signaling, and altering TGF-β/SMAD transcriptional programs.",
